## Quickstart

```bash
git clone https://github.com/NLievens/Grace-Data-Analysis.git
cd Grace-Data-Analysis
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

### Get the data

Raw and parsed GRACE/GRACE-FO datasets are not tracked in this repository
(too large for git). Download them from the **[Releases page](https://github.com/NLievens/Grace-Data-Analysis/releases)**
<!-- TODO: create the release, upload zipped Data/ subfolders as release assets,
     and update this link to point at the specific release tag if desired. -->
and extract into the `Data/` folder so the structure looks like:

```
Data/
├── Data_Reader.py
├── GRACE/
├── GRACE-FO/
└── Parsed_Data/
```

### Render a gravity map

```bash
cd Gravity_Maps
python main.py
```

Follow the CLI prompts to choose single-date, double-date, or baseline
heatmap rendering, and optionally set spatial resolution / geographic bounds.
Output is saved as PNG heatmaps and Excel exports under `Gravity_Maps/Saved Outputs/`.

### Run mass-change / EWH analysis

```bash
cd LSQR_Analysis
python main.py
```

Applies filtering and weighted least-squares regression to estimate mass
change (equivalent water height) over a selected region and date range.
Output is saved under `LSQR_Analysis/Output/`.

## Requirements

- Python 3.8+
- numpy, scipy, pandas, cartopy, openpyxl, geopandas, matplotlib

Install via:
```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

## Repository Structure

```
Data/            Raw + parsed GRACE/GRACE-FO data (downloaded separately, see above)
Gravity_Maps/    Spherical harmonic → gravity field heatmap rendering
LSQR_Analysis/   Filtering + least-squares regression for mass-change analysis
```

